
Examples:
	
	geedar -m:1 -i:"c:\test\input file.csv" -t:2  -k -c:P314C15L3R1
		This command line runs geedar in operation mode 1, processing
		satellite images that match the dates and sites in the file "input 
		file.csv" in the folder "c:\test", adopting a tolerance window of 2 
		days before and after the dates specified in the CSV file, defining 
		the areas of interest from KML files named as the sites in the CSV 
		and by applying a processing pipeline corresponding the product 
		#314, cloud algorithm #15, local algorithm #3 and reducer #1.

	geedar -m:1 -i:"c:\test\input file.csv" -t:1 -k 
	-c:[P314C15L3R1,P201C8L3R1]
		Same as the previous one, but now the time window is of one day and 
		two demand codes were provided, corresponding to two different 
		pipelines. The results of both will be combined in the output file, 
		which was not named in the command line and will, therefore, be 
		placed in the same folder of the input file with an automatically 
		generated name.

	geedar "c:\test\input file.csv" -t:1  -k -c:[P314C15L3R1,P201C8L3R1]
		Same as the previous one, but now both the '-m' and the '-i' were 
		omitted, which will make GEEDaR consider the first string as the 
		input file and use the file contents to automatically determine the 
		operation mode (if it corresponds to the mode 1 required columns, 
		that will be the operation mode).

	geedar "c:\test\input file.csv" -t:1 -r:250 -c:[P314C15L3R1,P201C8L3R1]
		Now the areas of interest will be determined as circles with radius 
		of 250 m around the geographic coordinates (decimal values) in the 
		columns "lat" and "long" of the input file.

	geedar "c:\test\input file.csv" -r:250 -c:[P314C15L3R1,P201C8L3R1] 
	-o:"c:\test\outuput\my_results.csv"
		Now the time window was not informed (it will be considered only 
		the exact dates in the input file) and the output file 	path was 
		manually defined.
